## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache invalidation design across multiple servers and cache backends, including stampedes, per-user invalidation, and pub/sub usage.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern
- Per-user key naming and TTL
- Invalidation triggers for writes
- Distributed locks (SETNX/EX) to avoid stampede
- Pub/Sub advantages with Redis vs lack of notifications in Memcached

## Code Example
```javascript
async function getFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  const cached = await redis.get(key)
  if(cached) return JSON.parse(cached)
  const lock = `lock:feed:${userId}`
  if(await redis.set(lock, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5)){
    const fresh = await computeFeed(userId)
    await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(fresh), 'EX', 300)
    await redis.del(lock)
    return fresh
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getFeed(userId)
  }
}

// Invalidation path (triggered by writes)
async function invalidateFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  await redis.del(key)
  if(usePubSub){
    await redis.publish(`feed-inval:${userId}`, 'invalidate')
  }
}
```

## Performance Characteristics

**REST**: HTTP/1.1 overhead ~1KB per request, limited to one request per connection. Best for CRUD operations with ~95% cacheability.

**GraphQL**: Single endpoint reduces network overhead, but query complexity can cause N+1 problems. Apollo Engine shows 30-40% payload reduction vs REST.

**gRPC**: HTTP/2 multiplexing enables concurrent streams. Protocol Buffers reduce payload size by 60-80% vs JSON.

## Use Case Scenarios

```typescript
// REST - Public API
GET /api/users/123/posts

// GraphQL - Mobile app with data requirements
query GetUserPosts($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    name
    posts(first: 10) {
      title
      comments(count: 3)
    }
  }
}

// gRPC - Internal microservice
service UserService {
  rpc GetUser(GetUserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}
```

## Error Handling & Authentication

- **REST**: HTTP status codes (200, 404, 500) + JWT/OAuth2
- **GraphQL**: Single 200 response with error payload + JWT
- **gRPC**: Status codes (OK, NOT_FOUND, INTERNAL) + SSL/TLS with token-based auth

## Real-World Applications

- Netflix: REST for public APIs, gRPC for internal services
- GitHub: GraphQL v4 API (95% faster than REST v3)
- Uber: gRPC for microservices communication (30% latency reduction)

